{
  "name": "unreadable-pause-clock",
  "about": "The one input that reaches the exporter's instant guard: a `human` pause whose clock cannot be parsed. The envelope's instants are the runtime's own `toISOString()`, so nothing writes this document today — which is exactly why the guard needs a fixture, because a guard no input reaches is a guard nothing holds honest. An instant this exporter cannot read degrades to a point inside the execution's window rather than to the epoch, where a collector would render the wait in 1970 (§12.4). The first pause loses its `pausedAt` and keeps its `settledAt`; the second keeps its `pausedAt` and loses its `settledAt`. That pairing is the point: a span's edges and the events stamped on them degrade to the same instants, so the placement §12.4 publishes — `human.paused` at its span's start, `human.settled` at its span's end — survives a clock nobody can read.",
